## Step_1: Initial Data Exploration
  The original dataset contains 80 rows and 7 columns. The Applicant name has 2 missing value, Email column has 4 missing values, the University column has 3 missing values. 
  There are 2 exact duplicate rows. The Application Date column is stored as text rather than a proper date datatype. Phone numbers have inconsistent formatting, 
  and some domain names and application statuses use inconsistent capitalization or spelling. These findings establish the initial data quality  before cleaning.

### Step 2: Handling Missing Values
I checked missing values separately for each column. 2 applicant names were missing, Phone, Domain Applied were missing in the original dataset
I removed the 4 records with missing email addresses because email is required for applicant follow-up. I filled the 4 missing University values with "Not Specified" 
to preserve the remaining applicant information. I used "Under Review" for any missing status values, status values were missing in this dataset. These decisions avoid unnecessary data loss while addressing the missing information.

## Step 3:  what I Found
I found 2 Duplicate rows and remove them using drop_duplicate().  Email is a more reliable identifier than a person's 
name because different applicants can share the same name. I kept the first occurrence of any repeated email address 
to avoid counting the same applicant multiple times.

## Step 4: Text Standardization
I removed unnecessary whitespace and standardized applicant names, email addresses, and application statuses. I created a mapping dictionary to convert different domain spellings into five consistent categories.
I also correct known university abbreviations where incorrect spelling. Finally, I checked the unique domain and status values to confirm that inconsistent capitalization and spelling no longer create separate categories.
This improves the reliability of grouping, counting, and analyzing applicant records.

